comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-10T12:59:45.637Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (branch fix/credential-shape-coverage): src/core/shared/secret-rules.ts gains a declared SecretStructuredSpelling on password_assignment (keys password|passphrase, element content) and high_entropy_assignment (quoted keys). The flat regexes are now composed from KEY + VALUE constants; a scratch comparison against HEAD confirmed all 20 rule sources and flags are byte-identical, so flat semantics cannot drift. The structured pass judges a quoted key's scalar value and every quoted string in value position inside an array/object under any key spelling, using the rule's own sticky value grammar (so the 8-char minimum and the <, ${, $( placeholder exclusions carry over). Bare tokens inside containers are never judged, which keeps password=[scrubbed] clean. First repo-wide scan found one false positive: tests/fixtures/telemetry/diagnostic-privacy.mjs:47 ('{\"password\": ', safe: true }) because the flat grammar's quoted alternatives cross line breaks; structured values are now bounded to one line (JSON/Python/YAML-flow literals never contain a raw line break). After that, node scripts/check-secrets.mjs is clean over all tracked files. Cost: the structured pass adds about 190 ms to a 196 MB / 24.5 s full-rule scan. The key cursor is cloned per scan because generator yields would otherwise share lastIndex between interleaved scans."
  "2026-11-10T15:06:46.712Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR 1701 (P2): a nested quoted key with a bare value inside a walked container, for example YAML password: {x: 'short', 'password': <value>}, was skipped because the key cursor jumped past the walked container. The key cursor now keeps scanning inside walked containers so nested quoted keys get their scalar check, while a walkedEnd marker ensures each container is still walked only once. Corpus gains the nested YAML and JSON cases. The pathological control still finishes in well under the bound (about 44 ms for the three nested-key inputs), and the fully nested detecting input now yields 2 findings (outer key and innermost quoted scalar)."
